Dear Education Attachés, Accreditation Officers, and Ranking Organization Representatives,

This is a follow-up to our January 21, 2026 correspondence forwarding Stanford Professor John Ioannidis's assessment of Korean university ranking fraud as an "addiction."

Since that email, our reciprocity audit has expanded significantly. Nine Korean universities now have documented false or unreciprocated partnership claims confirmed against the named partner institutions' own public directories. Not one has issued a rebuttal, a correction, or a counter-audit.

NEW: Chung-Ang University — 27% Reciprocity Rate

Our most detailed audit to date covers Chung-Ang University (CAU), Korea's self-described premier arts and film institution. Of 49 CAU claimed partner universities with publicly accessible directories, only 13 confirmed a partnership. 36 did not list CAU at all.

Notable absences:

Nanyang Technological University (QS #12) — CAU absent from their Korea partner list
UC Berkeley (QS #17) — lists KAIST, Korea University, SNU, and Yonsei; CAU not present
Duke University (QS #62) — South Korea country page entirely blank since at least 2020; Duke has publicly frozen all new bilateral undergraduate exchanges globally
Ohio State University (QS #190) — OSU's own records show Sogang-only exchange; CAU holds only a non-binding MOU

The prestige gap between verified and unverified claims at CAU averages −40 QS ranks — meaning the elite partnerships driving enrollment are exactly the ones that do not hold up to verification.

Full forensic report: https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/chung-ang-university-partnership-fraud-sexual-violence-doosan/

The Pattern Is Now Nine Universities Wide

CAU and Sogang University pulled their partner databases offline simultaneously on February 11, 2026 — the same day. Neither institution disputed the methodology. Sogang's database remained offline for weeks. CAU's was restored after 65 hours without any corrections or rebuttal.

Since then, Hongik University and Ajou University have also had their partner directories go blank, then return — again with no statement and no corrections. SNU's database has been dark since January.

Additional specific false claims now documented:

Keimyung University (Daegu): falsely lists Fudan, Beijing Foreign Studies University, Sun Yat-sen, and East China Normal University — all four absent from those institutions' own partner databases

CBNU and Catholic University of Korea: both list Universitas Indonesia as a formal exchange partner; UI's own database lists neither

SNU, Dongguk, and Ewha: all list the University of Oslo as an institutional partner; Oslo classifies all three as Faculty of Humanities agreements only — not institution-wide exchange

Complete nine-university documented record: https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/nine-universities-zero-rebuttals-korea-partnership-fraud/

What We Have Done — And What Comes Next

We have already directly notified the top 400 QS-ranked universities globally, alerting International Affairs offices to verify whether their institution's name appears on any Korean university partner list without a valid agreement. We are continuing through the top 1,500 QS-ranked institutions.

This notification campaign does not require any institutional announcement from any party to produce real consequences. As universities verify and find listed claims that do not correspond to active agreements, they will quietly decline to formalize or renew arrangements. Korean universities whose inflated partnership profiles are already under scrutiny will lose the ability to claim new affiliations at the tier they have historically inflated. Student exchange pipelines will dry up. The "International Outlook" scores that inflated their QS rankings will erode — without any press release, any legal action, or any formal finding.

The students enrolled under those inflated credentials will not be refunded.

In parallel, we are preparing outreach to international employers and global graduate recruiting agencies to notify them of the documented gap between the claimed prestige of Korean university credentials and the verified reality. Where institutions marketed elite-tier partnerships that do not exist, the degree issued to the student who enrolled based on that marketing was sold under false pretenses.

The Campus Safety Crisis These Institutions Are Also Hiding

Partnership fraud does not exist in isolation. It is consistently co-located with documented failure to protect students from sexual violence. We draw your attention to what our forensic reports have confirmed at three institutions specifically.

At Chung-Ang University, our investigation identified nine confirmed perpetrators across seven academic departments over a 21-year span (1998–2019). Not one received the harshest available sanction (permanent dismissal bar, 파면). The institution's own Human Rights Center recommended permanent dismissal in at minimum two cases — both recommendations were overridden or allowed to lapse without implementation. In one case, a professor was permitted to resign before any disciplinary procedure concluded and was subsequently rehired by a university overseas. In another, an adjunct instructor invoked a contractual loophole to avoid any formal disciplinary record being created at all. The pattern: every procedural off-ramp the institution could take, it took.

One professor's classroom conduct — documented in testimonies from multiple graduate students — included telling enrolled students that female artists must "endure" sexual contact from senior male faculty to succeed, and directing female students to allow a named colleague to touch them as a condition of professional advancement. This was transmitted in the classroom. It continued for at minimum six years before external exposure.

The full 21-year documented record, with primary source citations for every named case, is at: https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/chung-ang-university-partnership-fraud-sexual-violence-doosan/

At Sogang University, the structural issue is different but equally severe. Sogang is governed by the Jesuit Order — a male-only Catholic religious institution with undemocratic, permanent control over the Board of Trustees. In 2018, the Jesuit administration directly intervened to cancel a student-organized Human Rights Week speaker series after a feminist and LGBTQ+ educator was invited. A prior student newspaper published blank pages rather than submit to editorial censorship by the Jesuit foundation. The sitting president as of 2025 was re-appointed by the Jesuit-controlled board while under active criminal investigation for allegedly receiving approximately 1 billion won (~$730,000 USD) in "development funds" in exchange for appointing a donor's relative to an endowed chair position — after a police recommendation to prosecutors.

The structure at Sogang means that a student reporting sexual violence to an internal committee is reporting to an institution that has documented precedent for resolving serious misconduct entirely within the Order's internal processes, excluding students from proceedings, and suppressing the student press when it attempts to hold the institution accountable.

At Dongguk University — where this investigation began — the institution's response to documented sexual violence in its graduate film program was not a credible internal investigation. It was a delay of four months in issuing any discipline for the known perpetrator, followed by a pattern of systematic institutional protection. When Gender Watchdog began documenting the connection between the film program's sexual violence risk profile and the predatory access structure created by having a convicted bid-rigging criminal elevated to Dean, the institution's industry partner (Sidus FNH) responded by deploying a Korean law firm to issue criminal defamation threats within 48 hours. Under Korean law, stating a true fact can constitute criminal defamation. Dongguk has not responded to any direct outreach since April 10, 2025.

The Korean Women's Development Institute's (KWDI) 2020 national survey found that 61.5% of female students in university arts and culture programs experience sexual violence, with graduate film programs carrying an 81/100 risk score — the highest category in the study. Across surveyed programs, over 65% of perpetrators are faculty. These are the same institutions claiming elite global partnerships to recruit international students into these exact programs.

KWDI 2020 report: https://eng.kwdi.re.kr/inc/download.do?ut=A&upIdx=102748&no=1

The Worst-Case Scenario Has Already Happened

On October 28, 2025, Tu Anh — a 25-year-old graduate of Keimyung University's Department of International Commerce — fell to her death fleeing an immigration raid at a car parts factory in Daegu.

She was not working in a factory by choice. She was working in a factory because the "Global Degree" that promised elite connections to Fudan, BFSU, and Sun Yat-sen had secured her no E-7 professional visa pathway. The Korea Herald's own data confirms that only 10% of foreign bachelor's degree holders in Korea successfully obtain an E-7 visa. The other 90% are left on a D-10 job-seeker visa with no route forward.

Tu Anh is not a hypothetical liability scenario. She is the documented outcome when inflated credentials fail to deliver on their promises and students have no legal pathway out.

Full documentation: https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/deadly-fraud-did-ranking-inflation-kill-tu-anh/

